          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "28\tAt $\\beta{=}0.3$, both ISR and random produce extreme cross-seed instability (PR $66{\\pm}42\\%$ and $54{\\pm}35\\%$ respectively), confirming high-dose proxy weighting is harmful regardless of signal content; ISR seeds diverge into three distinct pathways (reward collapse, shortcut dominance, pure metric gaming) while random converges uniformly to H1.\n29\tThe effect transfers and intensifies in Prolog: 3/3 seeds yield 0\\% non-hacking via distinct mechanisms, including \\emph{query-as-fact gaming} that bypasses text-match classifiers.\n30\tISR's asymmetry is the central finding: \\emph{reliable as observer, unreliable as optimizer.}\n31\t\n32\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n33\tAcross 6 reward conditions, 3 seeds, 2 logic types, and up to 1000 steps, we contribute: (\\textbf{C1}) ISR as a diagnostic for GRPO reward hacking, with a 99.5\\% collapse trajectory invisible to PR and an SFT control localizing the failure to RL (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}); (\\textbf{C2}) empirical meta-level Goodhart, with a random control isolating ISR-specific gaming (69.7\\% vs.\\ 0\\%) and a premise-count-matched analysis showing the sole apparent gain is an LOO artifact (\\S\\ref{sec:goodhart}); (\\textbf{C3}) cross-logic transfer to Prolog with universal Goodhart (\\S\\ref{sec:crosslogic}); (\\textbf{C4}) an eight-mode hacking taxonomy including query-as-fact gaming and obfuscated tautology, motivating functional cross-validation of diagnostics (\\S\\ref{sec:taxonomy}).\n34\tThese are cautionary results for any work treating a structural proxy as a training signal.\n35\t"
            }
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "session": "worker_code_intro-tex-fix-and-compile_20260515T160708",
